<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rama III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1788">1788</a>–<a href="/years/1851">1851</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Nangklao</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> III, Phra Nangklao, was the third king of the Chakri dynasty of Siam, reigning from 1824 to 1851. His reign saw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burney-treaty">Burney Treaty</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1826, the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anouvong">Anouvong</a> of Vientiane in 1827–1828 and long wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>. A pious Buddhist and a successful trader, he enriched the treasury through the junk trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-pho">Wat Pho</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rama-iii.jpg" alt="Rama III" width="320" height="483" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Rama_III_of_Siam_(Nangklao)_Portrait_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
